I have an existing professional portfolio/business website built with HTML5, CSS3, and JavaScript.

I want you to convert this existing static website into a **fully functional custom WordPress theme with CMS functionality**.

IMPORTANT: Do NOT redesign the website from scratch. Preserve the existing UI, layout, responsiveness, animations, colors, typography, spacing, images, navigation, sections, and overall visual appearance as much as possible.

The goal is to convert the existing frontend into a professional WordPress CMS while keeping the current design.

==================================================
PROJECT OBJECTIVE
==================================================

Convert my existing:

- HTML
- CSS
- JavaScript
- Images/assets

website into a:

- Custom WordPress theme
- CMS-managed website
- Responsive website
- SEO-friendly WordPress website
- Easy-to-manage admin dashboard

The client should be able to update website content from WordPress Admin without editing HTML/CSS/JS files.

==================================================
CURRENT WEBSITE
==================================================

The existing project contains sections/pages such as:

- Home
- About
- Skills
- Portfolio/Projects
- Testimonials
- Contact
- Social links
- Footer

Analyze the existing project first before modifying anything.

Do not remove existing functionality.

Do not replace the design with Elementor or another page builder unless absolutely necessary.

Use a custom WordPress theme approach.

==================================================
WORDPRESS THEME STRUCTURE
==================================================

Create a proper WordPress theme structure similar to:

theme-name/
│
├── style.css
├── functions.php
├── index.php
├── header.php
├── footer.php
├── front-page.php
├── page.php
├── single.php
├── archive.php
├── 404.php
│
├── page-about.php
├── page-contact.php
│
├── template-parts/
│   ├── hero.php
│   ├── about.php
│   ├── skills.php
│   ├── portfolio.php
│   ├── testimonials.php
│   └── contact.php
│
├── assets/
│   ├── css/
│   ├── js/
│   ├── images/
│   └── fonts/
│
└── screenshot.png

Adjust the structure if the existing project requires a better architecture.

==================================================
PRESERVE EXISTING FRONTEND
==================================================

Before converting anything:

1. Inspect all existing HTML files.
2. Inspect all CSS files.
3. Inspect all JavaScript files.
4. Inspect all images/assets.
5. Understand the existing navigation.
6. Understand responsive breakpoints.
7. Understand existing animations/interactions.
8. Identify reusable components.

Then convert the static HTML into WordPress PHP templates.

Do not unnecessarily rewrite the CSS.

Do not unnecessarily rewrite JavaScript.

Do not change the visual design unless required for WordPress compatibility.

==================================================
HEADER
==================================================

Convert the existing header/navigation into:

header.php

Use WordPress functions such as:

wp_head()
wp_body_open()
wp_nav_menu()
body_class()

The navigation should be manageable from:

WordPress Admin
→ Appearance
→ Menus

or the modern WordPress navigation system if appropriate.

Preserve the existing mobile hamburger menu.

Make sure:

- Mobile navigation works
- Desktop navigation works
- Keyboard navigation works
- ARIA attributes remain correct
- Active menu item is identifiable

==================================================
FOOTER
==================================================

Convert the existing footer into:

footer.php

Use:

wp_footer()

Make footer information editable where appropriate.

Include editable:

- Copyright
- Social links
- Contact information
- Footer text

==================================================
HOMEPAGE CMS
==================================================

The homepage should become CMS-managed.

The following content should be editable from WordPress Admin.

--------------------------------
HERO SECTION
--------------------------------

Editable fields:

- Hero title
- Hero subtitle/description
- Primary button text
- Primary button URL
- Secondary button text
- Secondary button URL
- Hero image/profile image

Example:

Hero Title:
"Professional Front-End Developer"

Hero Description:
"I create modern, responsive and user-friendly websites."

Do not hardcode these values in PHP if they are intended to be editable.

--------------------------------
ABOUT SECTION
--------------------------------

Make editable:

- About title
- About description
- Profile image
- Experience
- Education
- Skills summary
- Button text
- Button URL

--------------------------------
SKILLS SECTION
--------------------------------

Make skills manageable from WordPress.

Each skill should support:

- Skill name
- Skill percentage/level if currently used
- Skill icon if applicable
- Skill description if applicable
- Display order

Do not create unnecessary complicated functionality if the existing design only needs simple skill entries.

--------------------------------
PORTFOLIO / PROJECTS
--------------------------------

Create a WordPress Custom Post Type:

Projects

Each project should support:

- Project title
- Featured image
- Project description
- Project category
- Project URL
- GitHub URL
- Technologies used
- Project date
- Project order

Admin should be able to:

Add Project
Edit Project
Delete Project
Publish/Unpublish Project

The homepage should automatically display published projects.

Use WordPress queries instead of hardcoded project HTML.

--------------------------------
TESTIMONIALS
--------------------------------

Create a manageable Testimonials section.

Each testimonial should support:

- Person name
- Person role
- Profile image
- Testimonial content
- Rating if currently used
- Display order

Use either a Custom Post Type or a suitable WordPress data structure.

--------------------------------
CONTACT SECTION
--------------------------------

Keep the existing contact form functionality.

The contact form should:

- Validate required fields
- Validate email
- Display loading state
- Display success message
- Display error message
- Prevent empty submissions
- Be mobile responsive
- Be accessible

If using Formspree, preserve the Formspree integration.

Do not expose private API keys in frontend JavaScript.

If a WordPress-native form implementation is better, explain the recommended approach before changing it.

Contact information should be editable from WordPress Admin.

Editable fields:

- Email
- Phone
- Address
- Google Maps URL/embed
- Social links

==================================================
SOCIAL MEDIA
==================================================

Create editable social media settings.

Support:

- LinkedIn
- GitHub
- Instagram
- Facebook
- YouTube
- Twitter/X
- Any other existing social links

Do not hardcode social URLs throughout multiple template files.

Store them centrally and output them dynamically.

==================================================
WORDPRESS CUSTOMIZER / SETTINGS
==================================================

Use WordPress Customizer, Site Editor settings, theme options, or a lightweight custom settings page where appropriate.

Do not install unnecessary plugins.

Create a clean admin experience.

The administrator should be able to manage:

Website logo
Site title
Tagline
Contact details
Social links
Footer information
Hero content
About content
Homepage content

==================================================
CUSTOM POST TYPES
==================================================

Where appropriate create:

Projects
Testimonials

Register them correctly using:

register_post_type()

Use:

- Public labels
- Admin labels
- Supports
- Rewrite slugs
- Featured images
- REST API support where appropriate
- Proper capability behavior

Use WordPress best practices.

==================================================
IMAGES
==================================================

Use WordPress media management.

Do not hardcode local image paths where WordPress media URLs should be used.

Use:

get_the_post_thumbnail_url()
the_post_thumbnail()
wp_get_attachment_image()
or appropriate WordPress functions.

Provide meaningful alt text.

==================================================
CSS AND JAVASCRIPT
==================================================

Load CSS and JavaScript properly through:

wp_enqueue_style()
wp_enqueue_script()

Do NOT directly hardcode CSS/JS links in templates if WordPress enqueue functions should be used.

Preserve the existing:

- CSS
- Responsive design
- Animations
- Hover effects
- Navigation behavior
- Mobile menu
- Scroll effects
- Sliders/carousels
- Other existing JavaScript functionality

Fix only issues that appear during WordPress conversion.

==================================================
SEO
==================================================

The original static website already contains SEO improvements.

Preserve the existing SEO implementation.

Make sure the WordPress version supports:

- SEO-friendly page titles
- Meta descriptions
- Canonical URLs
- Open Graph metadata
- Proper H1/H2/H3 hierarchy
- Image alt text
- Semantic HTML5
- XML sitemap compatibility
- robots.txt compatibility
- Clean URLs/permalinks

Do not create duplicate SEO tags if an SEO plugin such as Rank Math or Yoast SEO is installed.

If an SEO plugin is used, allow the plugin to manage metadata instead of hardcoding duplicate metadata.

==================================================
ANALYTICS
==================================================

Preserve the existing Google Analytics 4 integration if already configured.

Do not hardcode a fake Measurement ID.

Make the Google Analytics Measurement ID configurable.

Prefer adding the tracking code through WordPress's proper enqueue/settings mechanism or an appropriate integration.

Also consider tracking:

- Page views
- Contact form submission
- Important CTA clicks

Do not collect unnecessary personal information.

==================================================
SECURITY
==================================================

Follow WordPress security best practices.

Use:

esc_html()
esc_attr()
esc_url()
wp_kses_post()
sanitize_text_field()
sanitize_email()
wp_nonce_field()
check_admin_referer()
current_user_can()

where appropriate.

Never trust user input.

Do not expose passwords, API keys, private tokens, or credentials.

==================================================
PERFORMANCE
==================================================

Keep the website fast.

Do not add unnecessary plugins or libraries.

Optimize:

- CSS loading
- JavaScript loading
- Images
- Fonts
- WordPress queries

Use lazy loading for images where appropriate.

Avoid unnecessary database queries.

==================================================
RESPONSIVENESS
==================================================

The existing website is responsive.

Preserve the existing mobile-first behavior.

Test:

320px
375px
414px
768px
1024px
1280px
1440px+

Make sure:

- Navigation works
- Sections do not overflow
- Images remain responsive
- Text remains readable
- Buttons remain usable
- Forms work correctly

==================================================
ACCESSIBILITY
==================================================

Maintain or improve accessibility.

Use:

- Semantic HTML
- Proper labels
- ARIA only when necessary
- Keyboard navigation
- Focus states
- Alt text
- Sufficient contrast
- Accessible buttons
- Accessible navigation

Do not remove existing accessibility attributes such as:

aria-label
aria-expanded
aria-controls

==================================================
ADMIN EXPERIENCE
==================================================

The WordPress admin experience should be simple for a non-technical client.

The client should be able to understand:

Dashboard
Pages
Projects
Testimonials
Media
Contact information
Social links
Appearance
Settings

Avoid exposing unnecessary technical settings.

Use clear labels.

==================================================
NO PAGE BUILDER
==================================================

Do NOT use Elementor, Divi, WPBakery, or another page builder unless specifically requested.

The goal is a lightweight custom WordPress theme.

==================================================
PLUGIN POLICY
==================================================

Use as few plugins as possible.

Recommended optional plugins:

- Rank Math OR Yoast SEO — choose only one
- Formspree integration if necessary
- Google Site Kit if appropriate for Analytics/Search Console

Do not install multiple plugins that perform the same function.

Explain why each plugin is required.

==================================================
WORDPRESS BEST PRACTICES
==================================================

Follow WordPress coding standards.

Use:

get_template_directory_uri()
get_stylesheet_directory_uri()
get_header()
get_footer()
get_template_part()
wp_enqueue_style()
wp_enqueue_script()
wp_nav_menu()
register_post_type()
register_taxonomy()

Use hooks such as:

after_setup_theme
wp_enqueue_scripts
init
customize_register

where appropriate.

==================================================
IMPORTANT DEVELOPMENT RULE
==================================================

Do NOT simply convert every HTML file into a PHP file and hardcode all content.

The purpose of this task is to create a REAL CMS.

Content that the client needs to change should come from WordPress.

Static layout/design code can remain in PHP templates.

==================================================
FINAL ADMIN WORKFLOW
==================================================

The finished system should allow the client to do this:

Login to WordPress
        ↓
Dashboard
        ↓
Edit website content
        ↓
Update
        ↓
Website automatically reflects changes

For example:

Admin changes:

Hero Title
"Professional Web Developer"

to:

"Full-Stack Web Developer"

The website should automatically show the new title without editing code.

==================================================
MIGRATION
==================================================

Migrate the existing website content and assets into WordPress.

Do not lose:

- Images
- Text
- Links
- Portfolio items
- Testimonials
- Social links
- Styling
- JavaScript functionality

Use the existing website as the source of truth.

